  <h2 id="ai-and-law">What AI actually changes for a young lawyer</h2>

  <p>
    AI is genuinely useful in legal work right now for search, first-pass summarising of long judgments,
    and drafting a rough first version of a routine document. It is not yet a substitute for the human
    layer that still decides real cases and real deals: judgment on which precedent actually applies,
    negotiation sense, spotting the risk a template misses, client context, and final accountability for
    what gets filed or signed.
  </p>

  <p>
    A newer, genuinely useful layer here is legal-tech and AI-literacy for law: knowing how to use AI
    tools to speed up research and first drafts, and — just as important — knowing how to verify their
    output rather than trust it blindly. Courses on "AI, Law and Digital Ethics" or "Legal Research &
    Writing (Using AI)" are worth a look specifically because this checking skill is becoming a real,
    expected part of junior legal work, not a gimmick add-on.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Treat AI fluency as one layer of a wider <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> — domain
      knowledge plus AI-assisted speed plus your own verification judgment — not as a shortcut that
      replaces learning the underlying law. A law student who can use AI well and catch its mistakes is
      more useful to a firm than one who avoids it, and more trustworthy than one who cannot spot when it
      is wrong.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Do not pick a certificate course off a "top 10" list because it sounds impressive. Write down the
    specific role or practice area you are actually targeting over the next one to two years, and test
    interest in it for free first.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      If a free sample genuinely holds your attention through the unglamorous parts, and you can name the
      exact gap a paid course would close, that is the signal to spend. If you cannot name the gap, save
      the money and build proof through a moot, an internship, or a research assistantship instead — those
      routes cost time, not fees, and they build the same visible <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>
      that actually unlocks stronger offers and earlier financial freedom.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you want a second opinion on which specialization genuinely fits how you think and work, start with
    the free <a href={assessmentsUrl}>career and skill assessments</a>, or bring the specific decision —
    course, budget, and target role — into a <a href={guidanceUrl}>career guidance</a> session for a
    clear-headed read on your exact situation.
  </p>

